 ## Overview
-Volume Ray Casting with Marching is an image-order rendering technique that casts rays from the camera through a volumetric scalar field, sampling the field at discrete intervals to accumulate color and opacity using front-to-back compositing [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing 3D scalar data (such as medical CT scans or atmospheric density) by projecting it onto a 2D screen while preserving depth cues and transparency [S2, unofficial]. It integrates color and opacity along a ray [S3, unofficial]. Builds on the family principle of discretizing the Radiative Transfer Equation (RTE) along a ray [Volumetric and Atmospheric Rendering](4968-volumetric-and-atmospheric-rendering.md).
+Volume Ray Casting with Marching is an image-order rendering technique that casts rays from the camera through a volumetric scalar field, sampling the field at discrete intervals to accumulate color and opacity using front-to-back compositing [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing 3D scalar data (such as medical CT scans or atmospheric density) by projecting it onto a 2D screen while preserving depth cues and transparency [S2, unofficial]. It integrates color and opacity along a ray [S3, unofficial]. Builds on [Volumetric and Atmospheric Rendering](4968-volumetric-and-atmospheric-rendering.md).
 
