 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of samples along the ray, which depends on volume depth and step size [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of samples along the ray [G].
 *   **Memory Complexity**: Depends on the size of the 3D volume texture and the 2D colormap texture [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Performance**: Computationally intensive due to many texture samples. ERT reduces average samples for opaque regions [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths**: High-quality, physically-based volume rendering with accurate transparency and depth cues. Flexible for any scalar field [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Susceptible to aliasing artifacts ("wood grain") if not jittered; jittering causes artifacts on flat surfaces [S2, unofficial]. Performance degrades with large volumes or high resolution.
-*   **When to Use**: General-purpose volume visualization where quality is prioritized over extreme performance, or when ERT effectively culls opaque regions.
-*   **When Not to Use**: Real-time applications with very large volumes or strict performance constraints without optimization (e.g., hierarchical level-of-detail).
+*   **Limitations**: Susceptible to aliasing artifacts ("wood grain") if not jittered; jittering causes artifacts on flat surfaces [S2, unofficial].
 
